Windows Registry Editor Version 5.00

; Enables TLS 1.1 and 1.2 by default on the Windows versions that have them but
; do not use them unprompted, so that Sawbuck's https symbol servers work
; there. Windows 8.1 and later already do all of this; importing it there is
; a no-op.
;
; Every Windows from XP SP3 up can get to TLS 1.2. None of them below 8.1 is
; there out of the box, and the gap is an update plus these keys.
;
; The keys are identical on every version. What differs is the update:
;
;   XP SP3                    TLS 1.0 out of the box. TLS 1.1 and 1.2 come
;   POSReady 2009             from KB4019276, which is a POSReady 2009 update,
;   Embedded Standard 2009    so plain XP SP3 needs the POSReady enablement
;   Server 2003               first:
;                               HKLM\SYSTEM\WPA\PosReady  Installed = 1
;                             and then the update itself.
;
;   Vista SP2                 TLS 1.0 and 1.1 out of the box. The same
;   Server 2008 SP2           KB4019276 applies -- on Vista SP2 it installs
;                             behind KB4493471 as a prerequisite, the two
;                             sharing the 6.0.6002 servicing branch with
;                             Server 2008 SP2.
;
;   7, Server 2008 R2         TLS 1.2 is already in schannel -- on 8 it is
;   8, Server 2012            even enabled by default there -- but WinHTTP
;                             ignores it until KB3140245 is installed. That
;                             update is what teaches WinHTTP to read
;                             DefaultSecureProtocols at all, and it is
;                             offered for 8 and Server 2012 as well as for
;                             7, which is why the line is drawn at 8.1 and
;                             not at 8. Check with:
;                               wmic qfe | findstr 3140245
;
; So this file is the switch, not the installation. If the corresponding
; update is missing, importing it changes nothing and reports success, which
; is the confusing part worth knowing in advance.
;
; Run as administrator, then reboot -- schannel reads the protocol keys when
; the provider initialises, so restarting Sawbuck alone will not do it.
;
; Machine-wide, and not scoped to Sawbuck. Every WinHTTP application on the
; machine gains TLS 1.1 and 1.2, which is the intent, but it cannot be
; narrowed to one process.
;
; To undo it, delete the values rather than importing a mirror-image file: the
; original state is "value absent", which is not the same as "value zero".
;
; Note that Sawbuck does not need any of this. InitSymbolPath only fabricates
; a symbol path when there is no saved preference and no _NT_SYMBOL_PATH, and
; it picks http below Windows 8.1 precisely so that symbols work on these
; versions untouched. Its version check is about what it can know, not about
; what the machine can do -- it has no way to tell that this has been applied.
;
; So this file is for the case where you would rather have https anyway, and
; having applied it, the direct way to ask for it is to name an https server
; through the Symbol Path dialog or in _NT_SYMBOL_PATH. Either overrides
; Sawbuck's own choice completely.

; ---------------------------------------------------------------- schannel
;
; Both values are needed. Enabled turns the protocol on; DisabledByDefault
; stops it being skipped when the caller does not name a protocol, which is
; the case for symsrv.dll.
;
; Client only. Sawbuck fetches symbols and serves nothing, and enabling the
; Server side of a protocol is a separate decision with its own consequences.
;
; Under SYSTEM, which is not subject to WOW64 registry redirection, so there
; is one copy of these no matter the architecture of the process asking.

[HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Control\SecurityProviders\SCHANNEL\Protocols\TLS 1.1\Client]
"Enabled"=dword:00000001
"DisabledByDefault"=dword:00000000

[HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Control\SecurityProviders\SCHANNEL\Protocols\TLS 1.2\Client]
"Enabled"=dword:00000001
"DisabledByDefault"=dword:00000000

; ----------------------------------------------------------------- WinHTTP
;
; What WinHTTP offers when the application does not ask for a specific
; protocol. A bit field:
;
;   0x00000008  SSL 2.0        not set, and should not be
;   0x00000020  SSL 3.0        not set, and should not be
;   0x00000080  TLS 1.0
;   0x00000200  TLS 1.1
;   0x00000800  TLS 1.2
;
; 0x00000A80 is TLS 1.0 + 1.1 + 1.2. TLS 1.0 is included deliberately: this
; value replaces the default for every WinHTTP caller on the machine, so
; dropping it would break anything still talking to a server that offers only
; 1.0. Use 0x00000A00 for TLS 1.1 + 1.2 only.
;
; Both views are set, and on 64-bit Windows the second is the one that matters
; for Sawbuck: the x86 build is a 32-bit process, so its WinHTTP reads the
; Wow6432Node copy. Setting only the native key is the usual way this gets
; half-done. The Wow6432Node line is harmless on 32-bit Windows, where the key
; simply gets created and never read.

[HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\Internet Settings\WinHttp]
"DefaultSecureProtocols"=dword:00000a80

[HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\SOFTWARE\Wow6432Node\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\Internet Settings\WinHttp]
"DefaultSecureProtocols"=dword:00000a80
